Oral bioavailability depends on many factors: molecular weight, lipophilicity (logP), hydrogen bond donors/acceptors, polar surface area, rotatable bonds, etc. Typically, drugs with good oral bioavailability follow Lipinski's rule of five: MW < 500, logP < 5, HBD < 5, HBA < 10. Also, TPSA < 140 Å² is often associated with good oral bioavailability. Rotatable bonds < 10 is also a factor.

